00:01:45.080 --> 00:01:46.850
or provide your
own perspective.

00:01:47.670 --> 00:01:50.930
So you can raise your hand on
Zoom and I'll keep an eye out.

00:01:50.930 --> 00:01:53.550
You can put questions
in the Zoom chat.

00:01:53.550 --> 00:01:57.170
And feel free to just put a
little star next to it if it's

00:01:57.170 --> 00:01:58.985
something you want
me to read instead.

00:02:00.360 --> 00:02:02.990
And then we also have a
Slido link where you can ask

00:02:02.990 --> 00:02:04.370
questions anonymously.

00:02:05.430 --> 00:02:05.900
And Caitlin,

00:02:05.900 --> 00:02:08.810
if you don't mind sharing
that in the chat right now--

00:02:08.810 --> 00:02:10.130
Oh, Hannah got it already.

00:02:10.800 --> 00:02:12.110
Awesome, thank you.

00:02:12.860 --> 00:02:15.350
But you can ask
questions anonymously there as well.

00:02:16.500 --> 00:02:18.840
But with all that, thanks
again so much for joining us.

00:02:18.840 --> 00:02:21.890
I'm so excited to be
joined by my co-host today,

00:02:21.890 --> 00:02:27.260
Colonel Kris Saling, Director of
Innovation at US Army Human Resources

00:02:27.980 --> 00:02:28.760
Command.

00:02:28.760 --> 00:02:29.580
And Kris,

00:02:29.580 --> 00:02:32.090
I'd love to kick things off
with maybe having you introduce

00:02:32.090 --> 00:02:35.510
yourself, and sharing a little
bit about your role today,

00:02:35.510 --> 00:02:38.120
and something you like to
do outside of work too.

00:02:38.120 --> 00:02:38.930
- [CHUCKLES]

00:02:38.930 --> 00:02:41.225
No, that sounds good. And thank
you so much for that Rachael.

00:02:42.050 --> 00:02:42.870
So as you said,

00:02:42.870 --> 00:02:45.110
I'm the director of innovation
for the US Army Human Resources

00:02:45.110 --> 00:02:47.780
Command and everybody always tries
to figure out what that is.

00:02:48.410 --> 00:02:51.260
Innovation has become a buzzword.

00:02:51.260 --> 00:02:53.630
We always talk about
innovation theater and everything else.

00:02:53.630 --> 00:02:56.690
Our job is to be a little
bit of a future concept cell for

00:02:56.690 --> 00:02:59.810
the commanding general so that we
can be a little bit more

00:02:59.810 --> 00:03:04.100
removed from the daily asks and
tasks and figure out how we're

00:03:04.100 --> 00:03:06.050
going to take, and update,

00:03:06.050 --> 00:03:10.220
and sometimes transform our processes
to get after those future concepts.

00:03:10.220 --> 00:03:13.130
So I have a team that is
predominantly technologists. I

00:03:13.130 --> 00:03:15.500
have a lot of data scientists.
I have a cyber guy,

00:03:15.500 --> 00:03:16.700
have a networks guy.

00:03:16.700 --> 00:03:19.970
I have a human resources
digital transformation subject

00:03:19.970 --> 00:03:22.380
matter expert, and a
recruiting team. And

00:03:23.060 --> 00:03:25.340
that's just the start
of us. We're a year old.

00:03:25.340 --> 00:03:28.490
And I think we're going to
expand by another 10 positions

00:03:28.490 --> 00:03:29.870
or so this coming year.

00:03:29.870 --> 00:03:32.840
RACHAEL DEMPSEY: That's great, thank
you. And outside of work,

00:03:32.840 --> 00:03:34.070
what's something
you like to do?

00:03:35.030 --> 00:03:37.850
- I like to cook. So
to me, that's my zen.

00:03:37.850 --> 00:03:41.330
I'll put on some music and I'll
go in the kitchen and cook something.

00:03:41.900 --> 00:03:45.690
- Nice. So I know you said
this is a new team here.

00:03:45.690 --> 00:03:49.520
Can you share a little bit
about how the creation of that

00:03:49.520 --> 00:03:52.580
team came to be and maybe
how you work with some other

00:03:52.580 --> 00:03:54.185
departments across
the army too?

00:03:54.800 --> 00:03:58.155
- Yeah. No, it really started
when I was working in--

00:03:59.360 --> 00:04:00.630
just for some context,

00:04:00.630 --> 00:04:03.290
I've been working out of
the army people space for seven,

00:04:03.290 --> 00:04:05.540
going on eight years now,
which is kind of atypical.

00:04:05.540 --> 00:04:08.330
But a lot of what
we've been doing is digital modernization

00:04:08.330 --> 00:04:09.500
and transformation.

00:04:10.700 --> 00:04:12.560
Army came out with the Army
People Strategy, I want to say,

00:04:12.560 --> 00:04:17.000
in 2019 or early 2020 that
talked about how we wanted to

00:04:17.000 --> 00:04:20.060
modernize how we did
talent management. Pushed a lot,

00:04:20.060 --> 00:04:22.730
did a lot of different
pilots and prototypes.

00:04:22.730 --> 00:04:25.040
Got a bunch of policies changed.
We did some great stuff there.

00:04:25.040 --> 00:04:27.500
And then realized that
we hadn't modernized the

00:04:27.500 --> 00:04:30.950
mechanisms to actually get us
to talent management. We were still

00:04:31.730 --> 00:04:34.670
basically assigning people and
moving people around using the

00:04:34.670 --> 00:04:35.750
same old systems.

00:04:35.750 --> 00:04:39.420
And a lot of them really
needed to be updated.

00:04:39.420 --> 00:04:39.920
So

00:04:40.460 --> 00:04:43.610
we needed a way to look out
and see how we could do that

00:04:43.610 --> 00:04:47.330
cohesively rather than just
looking at the individual

00:04:47.330 --> 00:04:49.130
processes and the directorates.

00:04:49.130 --> 00:04:52.190
So General Drew made the
decision that he was going to

00:04:52.190 --> 00:04:57.485
establish the Innovation Cell to solidify
our HRC 2030 vision that we have

00:04:58.610 --> 00:05:01.010
and to look at how we're
going to step forward in our

00:05:01.010 --> 00:05:04.070
technology and our business
practices to achieve it.

00:05:04.070 --> 00:05:06.630
- I see, Catherine, you
had a question in the chat.

00:05:06.630 --> 00:05:07.730
Do you want to
jump in here first?

00:05:08.420 --> 00:05:08.510
CATHERINE:

00:05:08.510 --> 00:05:11.270
It was really just a question
on whether or not all of the

00:05:11.270 --> 00:05:15.080
people on your team are in
active duty in the military or

00:05:15.080 --> 00:05:17.150
if some of those are
also GS positions,

00:05:17.150 --> 00:05:19.430
or what your team
makeup looks like.

00:05:19.430 --> 00:05:21.080
- Yeah, it's military
and civilian.

00:05:21.080 --> 00:05:24.770
Right now we're mostly military
just because I took my team--

00:05:24.770 --> 00:05:27.860
I realigned my team that I
had for talent management with me.

00:05:27.860 --> 00:05:29.870
But we've made a couple
civilian hires and we're going

00:05:29.870 --> 00:05:30.890
to make a number of other

00:05:31.910 --> 00:05:32.900
civilian hires.

00:05:33.710 --> 00:05:38.585
- So a question I had
wanted to ask you about is

00:05:40.030 --> 00:05:42.280
Katelyn and I were talking
about how you've managed to

00:05:42.280 --> 00:05:45.670
kind of break silos between
the different army departments and

00:05:45.670 --> 00:05:48.847
getting people to work
together to help achieve the

00:05:50.010 --> 00:05:50.700
team's vision.

00:05:51.360 --> 00:05:52.275
And I was just wondering

00:05:52.890 --> 00:05:56.610
how have you been able to do
that and what tips do you have

00:05:56.610 --> 00:05:59.850
for some of us who are maybe
trying to do the same thing and

00:05:59.850 --> 00:06:02.010
break silos within
our own organizations?

00:06:02.970 --> 00:06:05.220
- I mean one of the
biggest skills that we--

00:06:05.220 --> 00:06:07.710
we harp a lot on
command in the military.

00:06:07.710 --> 00:06:09.930
But that's not the most
important leadership skill.

00:06:09.930 --> 00:06:11.850
One of the most important
things that we consider is

00:06:11.850 --> 00:06:13.290
building coalitions.

00:06:13.290 --> 00:06:15.690
How do you reach out to
your counterparts and get a whole

00:06:15.690 --> 00:06:19.500
bunch of people crashing
together on a common goal or a

00:06:19.500 --> 00:06:20.790
common understanding.

00:06:20.790 --> 00:06:23.280
I think one of the places
we've had the biggest success with

00:06:23.280 --> 00:06:24.900
that is with our data
literacy program.

00:06:25.590 --> 00:06:27.360
Because we had a lot
of people with needs,

00:06:27.360 --> 00:06:29.670
we had a lot of people who
are looking for more data.

00:06:29.670 --> 00:06:33.800
And we were able to pull people
from throughout the army to

00:06:34.560 --> 00:06:37.290
look at this as a project and
get it scaled up so now it's

00:06:37.290 --> 00:06:39.300
part of a professional
military education.

00:06:39.300 --> 00:06:39.900
- Thank you.

00:06:39.900 --> 00:06:43.080
I think Mike's question there
kind of touches on this as

00:06:43.080 --> 00:06:45.270
well. But Mike, do you
want to expand on that?

00:06:45.270 --> 00:06:48.285
- Yeah. And actually, I think
you answered part of it. But

00:06:50.115 --> 00:06:51.460
as you're building your team,

00:06:51.460 --> 00:06:53.710
and this is a kind of
question that we hear a lot at

00:06:53.710 --> 00:06:54.220
Data Science

00:06:54.220 --> 00:06:57.100
Hangout, do those team
members already have data

00:06:57.100 --> 00:06:59.830
science skills or are they
having to learn as they go?

00:06:59.830 --> 00:07:00.505
KRIS SALING: So we had a mix.

00:07:01.360 --> 00:07:03.910
One I hired straight out of
grad school out of a Georgetown

00:07:03.910 --> 00:07:06.780
Data Science Program. Another
I hired from Notre Dame

00:07:07.540 --> 00:07:09.220
in the Computer
Science Program.

00:07:09.220 --> 00:07:12.280
So they had a
number of different data skills.

00:07:12.280 --> 00:07:15.145
They had to cross-train into
other areas of emphasis.

00:07:16.390 --> 00:07:17.020
Obviously,

00:07:17.020 --> 00:07:20.530
natural language processing
and text string analysis is big

00:07:20.530 --> 00:07:21.580
when you're in
the people space.

00:07:23.180 --> 00:07:25.240
One of them came in with
a lot of those skills.

00:07:25.240 --> 00:07:26.290
The others are
having to learn it.

00:07:26.290 --> 00:07:28.990
And then I have a couple
other folks who were very good

00:07:28.990 --> 00:07:29.875
statistical analysts.

00:07:30.550 --> 00:07:31.050
Didn't

00:07:31.690 --> 00:07:34.900
have the same kind of
experience in building out some

00:07:34.900 --> 00:07:36.200
of the applications
we were building.

00:07:36.200 --> 00:07:37.820
So we're using a
number of different

00:07:38.740 --> 00:07:40.750
training programs, upskilling,

00:07:40.750 --> 00:07:43.810
pair coding to try to
get them set on that.

00:07:43.810 --> 00:07:48.020
Then I do have some folks who
come in straight from the HR side, very

00:07:49.660 --> 00:07:52.150
limited exposure to
data and data science.

00:07:52.150 --> 00:07:54.610
And it gave me an opportunity
to test our data literacy

00:07:54.610 --> 00:07:57.670
training on them to get them
to ask more intelligent data

00:07:57.670 --> 00:07:59.770
questions. Not that
they weren't asking intelligent

00:07:59.770 --> 00:08:03.490
questions, but there's less
translation needed between them

00:08:03.490 --> 00:08:05.455
and our data folks.

00:08:06.160 --> 00:08:08.240
- I'm sure I'll have follow-up
questions in a minute,

00:08:08.240 --> 00:08:08.830
but I'll come back then.

00:08:08.830 --> 00:08:09.670
[CHUCKLING]

00:08:10.510 --> 00:08:12.490
- Patrick, I see you just
put a question in the chat.

00:08:12.490 --> 00:08:13.780
Want to jump in here?

00:08:13.780 --> 00:08:14.920
PATRICK: Yeah,
thank you, Rachael.

00:08:14.920 --> 00:08:16.510
It's sort of a follow-up
to what you just said Kris.

00:08:16.510 --> 00:08:18.625
And thank you for being
here. I'm fascinated by this.

00:08:19.150 --> 00:08:21.130
You mentioned
text analysis NLP.

00:08:21.130 --> 00:08:24.700
What are your key data sources
for bringing that in to the

00:08:24.700 --> 00:08:27.640
analytic pipeline and then
also, other data sources,

00:08:27.640 --> 00:08:29.290
more quantitatively-focused
stuff?

00:08:29.860 --> 00:08:30.360
- So

00:08:32.110 --> 00:08:33.190
we use a number of different

00:08:34.630 --> 00:08:36.250
commercial products.
We use Hugging Face.

00:08:36.250 --> 00:08:38.409
We use a number of other
things you guys are probably familiar

00:08:38.409 --> 00:08:39.804
with to help us

00:08:40.510 --> 00:08:42.010
at least kind of break
down our taxonomies.

00:08:42.010 --> 00:08:44.560
But most of the work that
we're doing comes from our evaluation

00:08:44.560 --> 00:08:47.470
entry system where we
are looking at evaluations,

00:08:47.470 --> 00:08:51.205
we're looking at duty descriptions,
we're looking at how we

00:08:52.030 --> 00:08:55.810
establish our taxonomies
and ontologies for job

00:08:55.810 --> 00:08:56.890
competencies.

00:08:58.580 --> 00:09:01.270
There are a lot of libraries
out there that we could--

00:09:01.270 --> 00:09:03.790
but there's not really a
spacy library out there for

00:09:05.080 --> 00:09:07.150
military competency.
So we've had to

00:09:07.810 --> 00:09:09.040
practically build our own.

00:09:10.950 --> 00:09:11.810
Does that kind of
answer your question?

00:09:13.010 --> 00:09:14.570
My answer was all over
the place. [LAUGHS]

00:09:14.570 --> 00:09:15.890
PATRICK: No, that's awesome.

00:09:15.890 --> 00:09:18.300
And it's wonderful
work you all are doing.

00:09:18.300 --> 00:09:19.460
So thank you very much.

00:09:19.460 --> 00:09:20.330
- Oh, thank you.

00:09:20.330 --> 00:09:20.405
-

00:09:20.405 --> 00:09:23.060
And I go back to the
conversation about breaking

00:09:23.060 --> 00:09:26.150
down silos a bit, Luke
had asked in the chat,

00:09:26.150 --> 00:09:28.940
in such a gigantic operation,

00:09:28.940 --> 00:09:31.850
how have you been able to
actually break down those data

00:09:31.850 --> 00:09:33.930
silos to get what you need?

00:09:33.930 --> 00:09:34.070
-

00:09:34.070 --> 00:09:37.580
So the first step we had
to do was convince senior leaders to

00:09:37.580 --> 00:09:40.430
be directive. I mean,
it's horrible to say,

00:09:40.430 --> 00:09:41.960
you think we're going to want
to cooperate and share but

00:09:41.960 --> 00:09:43.580
nobody wants to cooperate
and share their data.

00:09:44.910 --> 00:09:47.850
So we went to Secretary
of the Army and said,

00:09:47.850 --> 00:09:50.750
I have a directive here that
says all the people in the

00:09:50.750 --> 00:09:53.370
people space will share
their people data, period.

00:09:53.370 --> 00:09:57.080
So it was our
first people analytics core directive.

00:09:57.080 --> 00:09:59.150
We've refreshed
that a couple times.

00:09:59.150 --> 00:10:02.720
And we've identified our person
event data environment as kind

00:10:02.720 --> 00:10:06.140
of being the semi-official
repository for that data sharing.

00:10:06.140 --> 00:10:09.110
We get a lot of people
who are working in that environment. It's

00:10:09.920 --> 00:10:11.980
probably something a lot of
you will be familiar with,

00:10:11.980 --> 00:10:15.750
it's just a massive
data warehouse with a virtual

00:10:15.750 --> 00:10:18.825
machine environment on top
of it that we use to drop

00:10:20.410 --> 00:10:23.560
different data sets and to
do different projects. But no,

00:10:23.560 --> 00:10:25.975
we had to basically order
everybody to share their data.

00:10:27.040 --> 00:10:27.115
-

00:10:27.115 --> 00:10:29.830
I'm trying to think how
that might translate to some of

00:10:29.830 --> 00:10:31.150
these teams here too.

00:10:31.690 --> 00:10:33.640
- Well, I mean, I think
the biggest piece,

00:10:33.640 --> 00:10:37.000
we talked about building
coalitions. There's going to be--

00:10:37.000 --> 00:10:39.550
we tried to do
some data summits

00:10:40.240 --> 00:10:42.490
across the army just to
bring people together and talk about

00:10:42.490 --> 00:10:43.790
what they were working on.

00:10:43.790 --> 00:10:44.260
And man,

00:10:44.260 --> 00:10:46.300
it would be really cool if
we could just get our hands on--

00:10:46.300 --> 00:10:47.800
well there's probably somebody in
the room who knows how to

00:10:47.800 --> 00:10:49.220
get their hands on that data.

00:10:49.220 --> 00:10:51.550
So we started this
little bit of a coalition

00:10:52.540 --> 00:10:55.090
talking about, wouldn't it be great
if we could do all this.

00:10:55.090 --> 00:10:58.546
And that's how we pitched the
argument to leadership to say, look,

00:10:59.800 --> 00:11:02.050
we've got to get here. We've
got to be able to do this.

00:11:02.050 --> 00:11:03.940
And we need your
support and endorsement.

00:11:03.940 --> 00:11:05.290
Ours was very directive.

00:11:05.290 --> 00:11:07.300
But I think if you
have a leader there who

00:11:08.020 --> 00:11:10.810
does give you that
champion, that support and endorsement,

00:11:10.810 --> 00:11:13.430
that might be a good corollary.

00:11:13.430 --> 00:11:17.290
- Definitely. Colter, I see
you had a question in the chat.

00:11:17.290 --> 00:11:18.610
Want to jump in here next?

00:11:18.610 --> 00:11:20.960
COLTER: Sure. And you actually
just kind of answered it.

00:11:20.960 --> 00:11:24.050
But I was wondering if
from your perspective,

00:11:24.050 --> 00:11:27.080
if you've seen similar data
modernization efforts happening

00:11:27.770 --> 00:11:29.420
either within other commands?

00:11:29.420 --> 00:11:32.780
I'm thinking of like a
Futures Command or even within

00:11:33.515 --> 00:11:36.350
individual branches within
the army if there are similar

00:11:36.350 --> 00:11:37.430
efforts going on?

00:11:38.110 --> 00:11:40.805
- Oh, yeah. No,
we've really got a,

00:11:42.020 --> 00:11:44.240
it warms my heart, data
modernization trend.

00:11:46.600 --> 00:11:48.130
Army Futures Command
is a big partner,

00:11:48.130 --> 00:11:49.390
their data science division.

00:11:49.390 --> 00:11:52.630
Nate Parker over there is a
big partner of ours in looking at

00:11:52.630 --> 00:11:55.750
how we can get more agile
data science. I hate to say agile.

00:11:55.750 --> 00:11:56.935
Not in terms of

00:11:57.640 --> 00:12:00.900
agile development, but something
that will flex better into

00:12:02.020 --> 00:12:03.640
our different requirements.

00:12:04.640 --> 00:12:06.760
So we're working with him
and seeing if we can use that

00:12:06.760 --> 00:12:08.500
instead of our
current environment.

00:12:08.500 --> 00:12:11.350
We've reached out to 18th
Airborne Corps who is using our

00:12:11.350 --> 00:12:13.915
Vantage Program which is
built on Palantir Foundry

00:12:14.650 --> 00:12:16.840
to do a lot of
their work, a lot of

00:12:17.650 --> 00:12:22.000
their data work informing
decisions. We've worked with

00:12:22.000 --> 00:12:26.350
the Army AI Integration Center that
is now up at Carnegie Mellon.

00:12:27.190 --> 00:12:29.350
We've got a lot of great
partners here. And West Point.

00:12:29.350 --> 00:12:30.375
I can't leave out West Point.

00:12:31.300 --> 00:12:32.320
Partner and good friend,

00:12:32.320 --> 00:12:36.130
Colonel Nick Clark who started
our data literacy training

00:12:36.130 --> 00:12:39.970
program that we've incorporated
and taken army-wide.

00:12:41.320 --> 00:12:45.130
And I think coming back to
finding those partners in the coalitions,

00:12:45.130 --> 00:12:48.270
as we figured out who all
was working in this space,

00:12:48.270 --> 00:12:50.360
we've been able to give each
other a hand up when we need

00:12:50.360 --> 00:12:52.310
when people need
resources or endorsement.

00:12:52.310 --> 00:12:54.570
And it's really made
things spread faster.

00:12:54.570 --> 00:12:57.860
- I know we touched on
the data literacy program a few times.

00:12:57.860 --> 00:13:01.520
And I was just curious
what does that program look like?

00:13:02.550 --> 00:13:02.710
-

00:13:02.710 --> 00:13:05.960
So the initial iteration was
just 10 hours of coursework

00:13:05.960 --> 00:13:09.275
basically making people better
critical consumers of data.

00:13:10.220 --> 00:13:13.460
Oh, hey, Nate's here.
Yes, always got your shout-outs.

00:13:14.240 --> 00:13:16.400
Making people better
critical consumers of data.

00:13:16.400 --> 00:13:18.980
That's expanded to about a
16-hour course that's going to

00:13:18.980 --> 00:13:20.105
be in our basic

00:13:20.870 --> 00:13:22.580
professional
military education.

00:13:22.580 --> 00:13:25.190
There's a second level that
they're going to get in that

00:13:25.190 --> 00:13:27.080
next iteration, which
is more hands-on.

00:13:27.080 --> 00:13:29.750
Using the common tools
that you have available,

00:13:29.750 --> 00:13:33.380
we'll start folks out just
doing basic statistical

00:13:33.380 --> 00:13:36.830
analysis in Excel or
whatever they have access to.

00:13:36.830 --> 00:13:40.220
I want to start introducing them
into R and Python so that

00:13:40.220 --> 00:13:42.170
it's not scary. We
can learn how to code. We

00:13:42.170 --> 00:13:43.710
share some of those
resources.

00:13:43.710 --> 00:13:47.690
So if they're interested or
if their branch has opportunities,

00:13:47.690 --> 00:13:48.770
they go into level three,

00:13:48.770 --> 00:13:51.500
which is where we take
them into some in-depth analytic

00:13:51.500 --> 00:13:55.410
training. That walks them
through our data competency model.

00:13:55.410 --> 00:13:59.030
And we basically create
learning paths for all the

00:13:59.030 --> 00:14:00.410
different things
that they want to do.

00:14:00.410 --> 00:14:01.653
If they want to get into

00:14:02.400 --> 00:14:05.160
different types of coding.
If they want to do more NLP.

00:14:05.160 --> 00:14:06.450
I need more NLP programmers.

00:14:06.450 --> 00:14:07.422
[CHUCKLES]

00:14:09.110 --> 00:14:11.030
That's always the fun part too
when you talk about NLP with a

00:14:11.030 --> 00:14:15.380
bunch of data scientists. You
know who your natural language

00:14:15.380 --> 00:14:18.230
processors are, and then you
know who your optimizers are.

00:14:18.230 --> 00:14:18.760
Because they're wait,

00:14:18.760 --> 00:14:20.982
what are you talking
about non-linear programs for?

00:14:20.982 --> 00:14:22.130
- [CHUCKLES]

00:14:22.130 --> 00:14:25.700
That actually leads me to
a question that was asked

00:14:25.700 --> 00:14:26.720
anonymously was,

00:14:27.440 --> 00:14:31.880
how much is R or Python
used in your organization?

00:14:32.580 --> 00:14:37.040
- So a majority of our
folks are Python users just because of

00:14:37.940 --> 00:14:42.455
our Vantage Program being built
on Foundry, which is PyCharm, PySpark.

00:14:43.700 --> 00:14:47.810
I've got a couple of folks
who do use R fairly regularly in

00:14:47.810 --> 00:14:49.130
doing their analysis.

00:14:49.130 --> 00:14:51.510
We had a lot of people on

00:14:52.190 --> 00:14:55.730
the more legacy programs
looking at SPSS SaaS.

00:14:55.730 --> 00:14:57.930
Not to disparage those
but they're expensive.

00:14:57.930 --> 00:14:59.780
So we want people getting
on things where they can more

00:14:59.780 --> 00:15:00.875
rapidly develop and share.

00:15:01.580 --> 00:15:02.360
My team,

00:15:02.360 --> 00:15:06.560
we try to use as much
of the existing language and share

00:15:06.560 --> 00:15:07.520
code as much as possible.

00:15:08.180 --> 00:15:10.940
But it gets difficult.
I think somebody--

00:15:10.940 --> 00:15:11.840
[CHUCKLES]

00:15:11.840 --> 00:15:13.250
I see Marlene's comment.

00:15:14.960 --> 00:15:17.810
I think somebody brought it
up earlier the sensitive nature of

00:15:17.810 --> 00:15:19.130
the data that
we're working with.

00:15:19.130 --> 00:15:19.940
I mean,

00:15:19.940 --> 00:15:24.020
most of our personnel data is
unclassified but it might as well be

00:15:25.240 --> 00:15:29.150
SEI-secured compartmentalized information
just because you

00:15:29.150 --> 00:15:30.350
have the

00:15:31.520 --> 00:15:32.580
personnel--

00:15:32.580 --> 00:15:34.310
trying to remember
what PII stands for.

00:15:34.850 --> 00:15:36.650
You have that component of
it. You have medical data.

00:15:36.650 --> 00:15:39.050
You have HIPAA protections.
You have legal protections.

00:15:39.050 --> 00:15:42.500
All these different caveats that
go around any kind of a

00:15:42.500 --> 00:15:43.930
soldier data object you create.

00:15:44.630 --> 00:15:47.910
So we want people being
able to use the tools--

00:15:47.910 --> 00:15:49.970
yes, Personally Identifiable
Information. Thank you, guys.

00:15:50.840 --> 00:15:52.070
RACHAEL DEMPSEY: I had
to go look it up too.

00:15:52.070 --> 00:15:56.300
Because I say PII so frequently
I forget what the acronym is.

00:15:56.870 --> 00:15:58.890
- No, we speak acronyms. It's
like, we know what it means.

00:15:58.890 --> 00:16:00.230
I just don't know
what it stands for.

00:16:00.230 --> 00:16:01.102
- [CHUCKLES]

00:16:02.850 --> 00:16:03.850
It's funny as

00:16:04.550 --> 00:16:06.230
we're covering some
of these questions,

00:16:06.230 --> 00:16:09.500
we get to a few of the others
that are asked in the chat

00:16:09.500 --> 00:16:14.510
here. But I know on this
topic of PII, Sita, you had a

00:16:14.510 --> 00:16:17.780
question in the chat on highly
protected data. Do you want to

00:16:18.470 --> 00:16:19.865
expand on that
with your question?

00:16:20.760 --> 00:16:21.260
SITA: Sure, thanks,

00:16:21.920 --> 00:16:25.805
Kris for answering some of
that already. But I guess,

00:16:26.660 --> 00:16:29.405
even talking about breaking
through these different groups,

00:16:30.390 --> 00:16:32.630
so different departments
I'm sure have different

00:16:33.380 --> 00:16:36.810
things that they consider
are protected or secure.

00:16:36.810 --> 00:16:41.270
So how did you deal with
that challenge of trying to be like,

00:16:42.260 --> 00:16:45.860
these are the kind of
tools we've built to ensure security

00:16:45.860 --> 00:16:49.700
of data within different
departments and to assure that

00:16:49.700 --> 00:16:53.240
this is not just all going
to be shared across every team or

00:16:53.240 --> 00:16:55.970
every group? And was
that challenging for you?

00:16:56.970 --> 00:16:57.470
- So

00:16:58.550 --> 00:17:01.070
we really spent a lot of
time working on our data governance,

00:17:01.070 --> 00:17:03.020
our policies, and
our protections.

00:17:03.020 --> 00:17:06.020
And we spent a lot of time
building that out in the person

00:17:06.020 --> 00:17:09.319
event data environment.
It's a secure environment.

00:17:09.319 --> 00:17:10.520
You can't download anything.

00:17:10.520 --> 00:17:12.740
You can't take anything
out of the environment.

00:17:12.740 --> 00:17:14.930
People get fussy about that
because it makes things hard to

00:17:14.930 --> 00:17:16.970
access and you've got
to request access through a

00:17:16.970 --> 00:17:18.050
project to certain things.

00:17:19.880 --> 00:17:20.380
But

00:17:20.900 --> 00:17:23.420
that at least, when
people are realizing that

00:17:25.609 --> 00:17:28.369
nobody's going to pull
everything into a CSV file or a

00:17:28.369 --> 00:17:30.320
spreadsheet that's going to
be sitting on their desktop,

00:17:30.320 --> 00:17:32.280
that provides a certain
amount of comfort.

00:17:32.280 --> 00:17:32.923
We also

00:17:33.630 --> 00:17:35.720
just restricted the access to

00:17:37.440 --> 00:17:39.540
any kind of identifiable
information for a lot of

00:17:39.540 --> 00:17:41.470
projects unless
it's specifically needed.

00:17:41.470 --> 00:17:43.500
So a lot of the work there
can actually be done with

00:17:43.500 --> 00:17:44.910
de-identified information.

00:17:44.910 --> 00:17:49.430
And we're trying to add
more information back into circulation now,

00:17:49.430 --> 00:17:51.960
get people more access now
that we've done the lockdown.

00:17:53.910 --> 00:17:56.730
But still, it's always a
little bit of a thing we watch.

00:17:57.390 --> 00:18:00.195
- There are so many
great questions piling in here.

00:18:01.170 --> 00:18:02.820
Marlene, I see you had

00:18:03.510 --> 00:18:06.270
a question in the chat. Do
you want to jump in here?

00:18:06.270 --> 00:18:06.945
- Oh, sure.

00:18:07.590 --> 00:18:09.045
Hi, Kris, thanks for
joining us today.

00:18:09.780 --> 00:18:11.190
I know. I'm so sorry, guys.

00:18:12.030 --> 00:18:13.995
Over beers, whoever's going to

00:18:14.700 --> 00:18:16.830
posit::conf. We'll
talk about my hiatus.

00:18:16.830 --> 00:18:18.330
But I've missed you all dearly.

00:18:18.330 --> 00:18:21.030
Even if we've met for
two seconds on these chats,

00:18:21.030 --> 00:18:23.710
just know that you guys are
an integral part of my week.

00:18:23.710 --> 00:18:26.250
So please take a little
hug for yourself in that.

00:18:26.250 --> 00:18:27.810
Anyway, so Kris, I digress.

00:18:29.610 --> 00:18:33.210
I'm just interested in what's
like a soft skill that you've

00:18:33.210 --> 00:18:36.030
started to really appreciate
from your team here.

00:18:36.030 --> 00:18:38.520
Because we always talk a
lot about the technical aspect and

00:18:38.520 --> 00:18:39.960
the skills that

00:18:40.680 --> 00:18:43.590
are attributed to being a
good programmer and/or data scientist.

00:18:43.590 --> 00:18:47.490
But I think there's so much
that has to be focused on about

00:18:47.490 --> 00:18:50.140
what aids in all of
that stuff, right?

00:18:50.790 --> 00:18:53.130
So what's a soft skill that
maybe you've kind of learned to

00:18:53.130 --> 00:18:54.420
appreciate more and
more with your team?

00:18:55.240 --> 00:18:55.320
KRIS SALING:

00:18:55.320 --> 00:18:58.230
So a lot of it unearthed itself
when we were doing our data

00:18:58.230 --> 00:19:00.180
literacy and it's
teaching. I mean,

00:19:00.180 --> 00:19:03.720
we have to have people who
have the soft skills that support

00:19:03.720 --> 00:19:05.190
being able to teach.
Now it's like,

00:19:05.190 --> 00:19:06.750
how can you connect
with somebody,

00:19:06.750 --> 00:19:09.930
how can you empathize with them
enough to be able to see where

00:19:09.930 --> 00:19:11.610
they're coming from
with their question.

00:19:11.610 --> 00:19:13.780
How do you put the

00:19:14.580 --> 00:19:16.890
process that you're trying to
describe or the result you're

00:19:16.890 --> 00:19:19.920
trying to describe in
a accessible context,

00:19:19.920 --> 00:19:21.640
kind of do those
understanding checks. I mean,

00:19:21.640 --> 00:19:23.500
it sounds very soft
in this context,

00:19:23.500 --> 00:19:26.520
but it's a critical piece
of being able to translate our

00:19:26.520 --> 00:19:28.380
solutions to a
decision maker who--

00:19:29.160 --> 00:19:31.065
If a decision
maker can't really

00:19:31.720 --> 00:19:33.850
use our data or our
decisions to understand it,

00:19:33.850 --> 00:19:35.020
it's a tree that
fell in the woods.

00:19:35.650 --> 00:19:38.440
So we've really emphasized
that teaching ability.

00:19:39.540 --> 00:19:41.840
- Yeah. And I think
something magical happens,

00:19:41.840 --> 00:19:44.310
I mean especially for
the rubber ducky programmers,

00:19:44.310 --> 00:19:47.170
when you're explaining a
problem to somebody else,

00:19:47.170 --> 00:19:48.180
I think some magic--

00:19:48.180 --> 00:19:49.440
Yeah, thanks, Libby.

00:19:49.440 --> 00:19:53.430
Some magic happens there
even for our understanding and/or

00:19:53.430 --> 00:19:55.800
our development of aha
moments. So anyway,

00:19:55.800 --> 00:19:58.860
thanks for that chiming
in. I'll pass the baton.

00:19:58.860 --> 00:20:00.350
Good to see you again,
Rachael, thanks.

00:20:00.980 --> 00:20:01.480
- Yeah.

00:20:01.820 --> 00:20:04.175
Thank you all for joining.
So nice to see everybody here.

00:20:05.360 --> 00:20:08.330
I'm scrolling up through some
of the questions and thank you

00:20:08.330 --> 00:20:10.880
so much to the Posit team
in the background helping me

00:20:10.880 --> 00:20:13.070
gather everything. But Ralph,

00:20:13.070 --> 00:20:15.060
I see you asked a
question a bit earlier.

00:20:15.060 --> 00:20:16.250
Do you want to jump in here?

00:20:16.250 --> 00:20:17.360
- Yeah, thank you.

00:20:18.110 --> 00:20:20.450
Morning. So Ralph
Asher. I'm a Marine.

00:20:20.450 --> 00:20:23.600
And just thinking about
my experience in entry level

00:20:23.600 --> 00:20:27.890
training a both as a student
and as an instructor in that,

00:20:27.890 --> 00:20:30.480
and it was very industrial,
and it was one size fits all.

00:20:30.480 --> 00:20:32.870
And I was curious if anything
that your team is doing is

00:20:32.870 --> 00:20:33.890
informing that,

00:20:33.890 --> 00:20:37.280
and even maybe before that
in the recruiting environment? Thank you.

00:20:38.170 --> 00:20:40.530
- Oh, goodness, I could
speak on that a lot.

00:20:40.530 --> 00:20:42.850
I would love to dig into how
we structure our professional

00:20:42.850 --> 00:20:46.930
military education so that
it's not the one size fits all.

00:20:48.730 --> 00:20:51.880
The way we're rolling out
the different data competencies, I think,

00:20:51.880 --> 00:20:54.910
is going to help a little
bit with that because one of the

00:20:54.910 --> 00:20:58.090
things we wanted to do was
have all kinds of different levels,

00:20:58.090 --> 00:20:59.740
all kinds of different
learning paths,

00:20:59.740 --> 00:21:01.480
figure out how to
make this accessible,

00:21:01.480 --> 00:21:03.250
and how to make this
apply to people's--

00:21:03.250 --> 00:21:05.080
what are they going
to use the most.

00:21:05.080 --> 00:21:08.200
Then we front load that to
give them the confidence and

00:21:08.200 --> 00:21:09.205
competence they need.

00:21:10.660 --> 00:21:11.650
Sometimes,

00:21:11.650 --> 00:21:14.500
I mean that's just the
conceptual piece of how to ask

00:21:14.500 --> 00:21:15.580
a good data question.

00:21:15.580 --> 00:21:18.670
And we workshop with people
through what that looks like.

00:21:18.670 --> 00:21:22.870
There's a lot of data
storytelling and how to read

00:21:22.870 --> 00:21:24.610
graphs, how to read
statistics, how to be

00:21:25.570 --> 00:21:28.600
a good critical consumer of all of
the things that we see in the news.

00:21:29.770 --> 00:21:31.110
Then we start getting into

00:21:32.110 --> 00:21:34.765
some of the more
hands-on skills. But

00:21:35.710 --> 00:21:36.490
at your question--

00:21:36.490 --> 00:21:37.520
in terms of your question,

00:21:37.520 --> 00:21:39.665
that's something I would
like to go after I think

00:21:40.880 --> 00:21:43.190
our Combined Arms Center
Commander General Beagle is

00:21:43.190 --> 00:21:46.400
going after that. But
it's a work in progress.

00:21:46.400 --> 00:21:47.165
[CHUCKLES]

00:21:47.165 --> 00:21:49.250
- And I see Alan
you had a question

00:21:50.180 --> 00:21:53.840
about the different teams
working together and we'd love

00:21:53.840 --> 00:21:54.845
to have you ask that next.

00:21:55.370 --> 00:21:58.965
- Yeah, sure. Thanks, Rachael.
Hi, Kris. I'm curious,

00:21:59.840 --> 00:22:02.600
in my experience it's hard
sometimes to be able to

00:22:03.100 --> 00:22:06.530
celebrate achievements or
innovative things that folks on

00:22:06.530 --> 00:22:09.140
my team do because their
sensitivity about how much we

00:22:09.140 --> 00:22:11.520
can share sometimes
even internally,

00:22:11.520 --> 00:22:14.630
but for sure externally with
the broader world of data

00:22:14.630 --> 00:22:16.130
engineering and data science.

00:22:16.130 --> 00:22:19.400
And I imagine that's really acute
for you but I don't know

00:22:19.400 --> 00:22:20.690
if that's the right assumption.

00:22:20.690 --> 00:22:25.640
I'm curious if you've got ways
to manage that and deal with

00:22:25.640 --> 00:22:27.920
that sort of dilemma of how
do you help your team feel like

00:22:27.920 --> 00:22:32.840
they belong to a community
but keep the right borders between

00:22:33.704 --> 00:22:36.530
oversharing and keeping the
right level of discretion,

00:22:36.530 --> 00:22:37.550
and sensitivity, and stuff?

00:22:38.670 --> 00:22:40.470
- Yeah, no, that is
an excellent question.

00:22:40.470 --> 00:22:43.840
Because one of the things that I've
had to do over the past two, three,

00:22:43.840 --> 00:22:46.890
four years is be the army's
spokesperson for people

00:22:46.890 --> 00:22:48.360
analytics and how
we're using data,

00:22:48.360 --> 00:22:49.620
how we're modernizing
promotions,

00:22:49.620 --> 00:22:50.760
how we're doing all this stuff.

00:22:50.760 --> 00:22:53.880
A lot of that is extremely
sensitive and it's hard being

00:22:53.880 --> 00:22:56.260
able to talk about
what we're doing,

00:22:56.260 --> 00:22:59.790
especially to an expert
audience when we can't go into

00:22:59.790 --> 00:23:02.655
the nuts and bolts of how
we built out the particular program.

00:23:03.270 --> 00:23:07.800
We've really tried to put
together the nuts and bolts in

00:23:07.800 --> 00:23:09.390
terms of competencies.

00:23:09.390 --> 00:23:12.480
We can talk all day about
how we use text string analysis to

00:23:12.480 --> 00:23:15.810
automate the initial screening
for command selection now.

00:23:15.810 --> 00:23:18.840
Don't necessarily tell you
the rubric that we're using to

00:23:18.840 --> 00:23:21.600
judge these things because we
don't want that to necessarily

00:23:21.600 --> 00:23:23.190
get out. That's kind
of a rough example.

00:23:23.190 --> 00:23:24.330
But we

00:23:25.110 --> 00:23:26.130
do try to be transparent.

00:23:26.130 --> 00:23:28.290
And part of being transparent
with the force about

00:23:28.890 --> 00:23:30.970
what we're doing
with their data,

00:23:30.970 --> 00:23:32.610
especially when it's
their personal data,

00:23:33.300 --> 00:23:34.350
is important.

00:23:35.060 --> 00:23:38.845
I don't know if that's a
more of a non-answer but

00:23:39.730 --> 00:23:40.750
I kind of tap dance around it.

00:23:40.750 --> 00:23:42.850
We try to talk about
as much as we can.

00:23:42.850 --> 00:23:43.350
- Yeah,

00:23:45.070 --> 00:23:48.070
it's interesting to know that
you've got the same dilemma.

00:23:48.070 --> 00:23:50.230
And I think I saw
Mark pop up the note,

00:23:50.230 --> 00:23:53.170
that kind of question probably holds
for lots of HR analytics stuff

00:23:53.770 --> 00:23:54.025
broadly.

00:23:54.025 --> 00:23:54.550
Yep.

00:23:54.550 --> 00:23:54.610
-

00:23:54.610 --> 00:23:56.980
It applies for us in areas
like R&amp;amp;D and how we solve

00:23:56.980 --> 00:23:59.560
manufacturing problems and stuff
like that as well.

00:23:59.560 --> 00:24:02.050
Tricky to figure out that
right balance. So appreciate it. Thanks.

00:24:02.890 --> 00:24:03.340
KRIS SALING: No worries.

00:24:03.340 --> 00:24:07.180
We do try to come up with mock
up versions of some of the

00:24:07.180 --> 00:24:09.790
algorithms we use that we
can put publicly on Git.

00:24:09.790 --> 00:24:13.270
So like there is a
publicly accessible version of the

00:24:13.270 --> 00:24:14.860
retention prediction
model we use.

00:24:14.860 --> 00:24:18.880
Just the base interval
forecasting engine out on Git.

00:24:18.880 --> 00:24:20.920
So the more I think
we can develop those.

00:24:20.920 --> 00:24:24.010
Then we also have multi-purpose
models and we can get the

00:24:24.010 --> 00:24:27.220
community to critique how we
put it together in the methodology.

00:24:27.850 --> 00:24:29.530
- Cool, neat. Thank you.

00:24:30.790 --> 00:24:33.770
- I imagine that link to that

00:24:34.450 --> 00:24:37.600
Git example is going to
be something people are interested in.

00:24:37.600 --> 00:24:38.980
KRIS SALING: Yeah,
as soon as I can,

00:24:38.980 --> 00:24:40.390
I'll shoot it over to you cool.

00:24:40.390 --> 00:24:41.170
You can share it
with the group. RACHAEL

00:24:41.170 --> 00:24:43.675
DEMPSEY: Yeah, I can put it
up with the recording too.

00:24:44.530 --> 00:24:45.750
Awesome. Catherine,

00:24:45.750 --> 00:24:50.380
I know you had a question that
touched on what Alan said but I

00:24:50.380 --> 00:24:53.170
just wanted to make sure to see
if you wanted to expand on that

00:24:53.170 --> 00:24:56.260
a bit or if that answered
your question too.

00:24:56.840 --> 00:24:56.880
CATHERINE:

00:24:56.880 --> 00:24:59.650
My question of centers around the
fact that this is not a

00:24:59.650 --> 00:25:02.710
problem unique to
the army or any

00:25:04.060 --> 00:25:05.860
industry in general.
But is there

00:25:06.490 --> 00:25:10.530
some sort of higher level
cross-branch innovation for HR,

00:25:11.170 --> 00:25:14.200
people leaders that maybe
your team is a part of?

00:25:14.200 --> 00:25:17.830
Or do you share any of
that with the Air Force, the Navy,

00:25:17.830 --> 00:25:21.460
any of those other kinds of
military organizations that you

00:25:21.460 --> 00:25:22.810
might have access to?

00:25:22.810 --> 00:25:23.590
- Yeah, so

00:25:24.510 --> 00:25:27.040
we reached out to
AppWorks and Softworks.

00:25:27.040 --> 00:25:31.360
A lot of their innovation is
not necessarily in how they're building--

00:25:31.360 --> 00:25:33.820
they do build a lot of
prototypes the same way we do

00:25:33.820 --> 00:25:35.335
for different tools
we're trying to roll out.

00:25:36.460 --> 00:25:38.320
But a lot in the
contracting space,

00:25:38.320 --> 00:25:41.050
we've had to pull in
expertise in that.

00:25:41.050 --> 00:25:45.040
We've talked with the Navy
director of military personnel Management.

00:25:45.040 --> 00:25:47.380
And he's very interested in
sharing some of the things that

00:25:47.380 --> 00:25:51.040
they've done for my Navy HR
with us and getting a readout

00:25:51.040 --> 00:25:54.760
on what we've been doing
for automating and updating our

00:25:54.760 --> 00:25:56.260
promotions and performance.

00:25:58.300 --> 00:26:00.220
We have a lot of--

00:26:00.220 --> 00:26:02.050
and I think this is
probably common everywhere.

00:26:02.050 --> 00:26:04.465
We have a lot of these
little networks built

00:26:05.290 --> 00:26:07.765
but it's very unofficial. I
don't think you could make

00:26:08.410 --> 00:26:10.330
a official innovation
network. Although,

00:26:10.330 --> 00:26:13.180
we are tied in with Defense
Innovation Unit and the

00:26:13.180 --> 00:26:16.540
National Security Innovation Network
for some of the things

00:26:16.540 --> 00:26:17.450
that we're working on.

00:26:17.450 --> 00:26:19.615
But the rest of it is
just point to point.

00:26:20.170 --> 00:26:22.120
It's a small space
we're working in,

00:26:22.120 --> 00:26:25.105
at least on the DOD
side in the army side.

00:26:26.320 --> 00:26:31.400
It's a small enough space that we work
in that we know who's working on it.

00:26:31.400 --> 00:26:34.060
And if we don't know
somebody personally who's working on a

00:26:34.060 --> 00:26:36.430
particular problem, someone
in that network does.

00:26:37.160 --> 00:26:37.930
So we've been lucky.

00:26:38.590 --> 00:26:39.130
- Thank you.

00:26:39.750 --> 00:26:41.635
I see there was an
anonymous question

00:26:42.520 --> 00:26:45.040
that said apart from
the NLP that you shared,

00:26:45.040 --> 00:26:48.085
what are some other typical
analysis that you do?

00:26:48.740 --> 00:26:50.440
- Oops, I lost you
for just a second.

00:26:50.440 --> 00:26:51.730
Can you repeat the last
part of that question.

00:26:51.730 --> 00:26:55.120
- Sorry. The question was,
apart from the NLP example,

00:26:55.120 --> 00:26:57.625
what are some other typical
analysis that you do?

00:26:58.690 --> 00:27:01.672
- Well, one of the programs
that we're working on is just

00:27:02.820 --> 00:27:04.550
a big neural net
that takes in all our

00:27:05.250 --> 00:27:07.710
personnel data and rolls out

00:27:08.460 --> 00:27:10.560
a prediction vector of how
likely someone is to retreat

00:27:10.560 --> 00:27:11.475
from service over time.

00:27:12.400 --> 00:27:14.550
So we do a lot of
retention analysis.

00:27:17.520 --> 00:27:21.660
We're trying to figure out
how to improve on an existing

00:27:21.660 --> 00:27:24.975
simulation that shows how our
distribution could get backed up.

00:27:26.520 --> 00:27:29.505
We want to try to
forecast what our

00:27:30.810 --> 00:27:33.270
force is going to look like
or our force inventory is going to

00:27:33.270 --> 00:27:34.200
look like over 5 years.

00:27:34.200 --> 00:27:37.380
Because obviously you probably heard
in the news about the

00:27:37.380 --> 00:27:38.370
recruiting issues.

00:27:39.330 --> 00:27:41.175
Issues sounds too kind. But

00:27:42.000 --> 00:27:43.800
I'm on camera so I'll just
say recruiting issues.

00:27:43.800 --> 00:27:47.100
But that's going to leave a little
bit of a trough in the force.

00:27:47.100 --> 00:27:47.600
Even if

00:27:48.150 --> 00:27:52.230
we recover from it in the
very near future, it's still going

00:27:52.230 --> 00:27:55.590
to be something that we have
to build our inventory around over

00:27:55.590 --> 00:27:57.370
the next 5-0 years.

00:27:57.370 --> 00:28:01.310
So we want to be able to
take a look at what that looks like

00:28:01.310 --> 00:28:06.050
and prioritize skill
development and assignments accordingly.

00:28:06.050 --> 00:28:08.470
So we're trying to build
some of those forecasting models.

00:28:08.470 --> 00:28:08.970
Then

00:28:10.060 --> 00:28:12.910
we're trying to work with
a number of different elements,

00:28:12.910 --> 00:28:16.150
working with the Army Software
Factory on just getting data

00:28:16.150 --> 00:28:18.760
shared into applications that'll
make it easier for

00:28:18.760 --> 00:28:23.160
folks to do things like finding
childcare, PCS, et cetera.

00:28:23.950 --> 00:28:26.290
Permanent Change of Station. I
don't want to speak an acronym.

00:28:26.290 --> 00:28:27.106
[CHUCKLES]

00:28:28.330 --> 00:28:29.215
- Thank you very much.

00:28:31.650 --> 00:28:35.040
Let's see. I feel like I'm
firing questions away at you.

00:28:35.040 --> 00:28:37.480
We have so many
great questions. Let's see.

00:28:37.480 --> 00:28:39.390
- No, they're asking some
great stuff. This is fun.

00:28:39.390 --> 00:28:40.190
- [CHUCKLES]

00:28:41.760 --> 00:28:44.410
Oh, let's jump over to your
question, [? Dipsha. ?]

00:28:44.410 --> 00:28:45.390
Do you to jump in here?

00:28:46.380 --> 00:28:46.880
- Sure.

00:28:47.400 --> 00:28:49.080
Thanks, Rachael. Thanks, Kris.

00:28:49.080 --> 00:28:51.370
I think a lot of it
you've already answered.

00:28:51.370 --> 00:28:53.880
But as you started
to build your team,

00:28:53.880 --> 00:28:57.250
are there success stories that
you were able to share with

00:28:57.990 --> 00:29:01.800
your leadership or other teams
that you're trying to get data

00:29:01.800 --> 00:29:05.610
from to say like, look what
we're capable of if you

00:29:05.610 --> 00:29:08.760
cooperated to get that
momentum and buy in going?

00:29:09.720 --> 00:29:10.230
- Yeah,

00:29:10.230 --> 00:29:13.770
that was one of the biggest things
we had to do initially was to show

00:29:14.670 --> 00:29:16.170
value. I mean we wanted to

00:29:17.280 --> 00:29:19.360
find projects that were kind of
quick wins. That we could say,

00:29:19.360 --> 00:29:22.430
hey, look how much easier this
is to answer with data. And

00:29:24.240 --> 00:29:26.025
trying to make that
business case to leaders.

00:29:27.100 --> 00:29:31.320
One of the things that we used.
I worked for about two years on

00:29:31.320 --> 00:29:33.300
a structure to personnel

00:29:33.990 --> 00:29:35.070
mismatch program.

00:29:35.640 --> 00:29:37.710
Because we were trying to
figure out with all the rapid

00:29:37.710 --> 00:29:39.930
changes for Army structure,

00:29:39.930 --> 00:29:43.860
why did we have a force
that looked very different from the

00:29:43.860 --> 00:29:45.180
structure that we required.

00:29:45.180 --> 00:29:46.930
And the answer was obviously,

00:29:46.930 --> 00:29:48.700
the senior leaders can say I
want to build this type of

00:29:48.700 --> 00:29:50.980
organization. The
requirements are built.

00:29:50.980 --> 00:29:53.480
And it takes much longer
for the supply to catch up.

00:29:53.480 --> 00:29:56.530
So as an argument for
data-driven talent management

00:29:56.530 --> 00:29:59.350
where we look at
attribute-based assignment

00:29:59.350 --> 00:30:02.365
versus branch and a grade,

00:30:03.520 --> 00:30:07.720
look how much we can reduce the gap
in what we have versus what we assign.

00:30:07.720 --> 00:30:08.860
Because we have
these skills here,

00:30:08.860 --> 00:30:10.090
they're just coded
under another

00:30:11.050 --> 00:30:15.220
specialty. If we make some
of these positions more immaterial,

00:30:15.220 --> 00:30:17.350
then we have a lot more
assignment options and the

00:30:17.350 --> 00:30:19.880
ability to fill that
talent requirement.

00:30:19.880 --> 00:30:23.500
So that one was fairly easy.
We did it with existing data,

00:30:23.500 --> 00:30:25.390
existing position analysis.

00:30:25.390 --> 00:30:27.640
We have much more in-depth
understanding of the

00:30:27.640 --> 00:30:28.585
requirements now.

00:30:29.200 --> 00:30:31.840
But we were able to show
very quickly. It's like, OK,

00:30:31.840 --> 00:30:34.930
all of these stationing issues
that we think initially were a

00:30:34.930 --> 00:30:37.120
problem, much
less of a problem.

00:30:37.120 --> 00:30:38.880
- It's a great example.
Thank you, Kris.

00:30:39.670 --> 00:30:40.170
- Thank you.

00:30:40.720 --> 00:30:42.010
RACHAEL DEMPSEY:
Yeah, thank you. And I

00:30:43.090 --> 00:30:44.200
echo [? Arian's ?]

00:30:44.200 --> 00:30:44.740
comment,.

00:30:44.740 --> 00:30:48.040
Love the idea of quick wins
projects and spending some time

00:30:48.040 --> 00:30:51.730
thinking about what those could
be in our own organizations too.

00:30:52.840 --> 00:30:54.370
Brad, I see you have
your hand raised here.

00:30:54.370 --> 00:30:55.300
Do you want to jump in?

00:30:55.930 --> 00:30:56.430
BRAD: Sure.

00:30:56.940 --> 00:31:00.990
A lot of focus is being paid
to AI and machine learning,

00:31:00.990 --> 00:31:04.230
and data mesh, and data fabric,
and those are, I feel like,

00:31:04.230 --> 00:31:04.960
advanced concepts,

00:31:05.640 --> 00:31:09.330
like the XYZ in the alphabet.
And if you don't do the ABCs,

00:31:09.330 --> 00:31:11.385
the XYZs aren't going
to turn out very well.

00:31:12.030 --> 00:31:14.880
Do you guys currently
have a platform around

00:31:15.600 --> 00:31:19.650
either data catalog or
governance that helps people

00:31:19.650 --> 00:31:21.750
find the right data at
the right time, and the right

00:31:21.750 --> 00:31:24.555
reason, and use
it appropriately?

00:31:24.555 --> 00:31:25.055
- So

00:31:26.100 --> 00:31:27.190
we've rolled one out.

00:31:27.190 --> 00:31:29.340
It doesn't have the use that I
would like it to have. It's the

00:31:30.705 --> 00:31:31.205
EDSC,

00:31:33.450 --> 00:31:35.400
Enterprise Data
Sytem Catalog. So

00:31:36.330 --> 00:31:39.240
it should have everything
registered in it that we have

00:31:39.240 --> 00:31:40.750
and we're collecting. And

00:31:41.490 --> 00:31:43.680
if we could get more
people accessing it,

00:31:43.680 --> 00:31:45.840
I think that would go toward
a lot of our problems.

00:31:45.840 --> 00:31:49.680
The hard part is we're not
using the tool directly in with

00:31:49.680 --> 00:31:51.790
the data systems where
that data is stored.

00:31:51.790 --> 00:31:53.800
So we have to
manually update it.

00:31:53.800 --> 00:31:55.990
But it's something
that we've considered.

00:31:55.990 --> 00:31:57.850
We've identified
that as a need. And

00:32:00.400 --> 00:32:02.920
one of the things I'm looking
for is a more intelligent data

00:32:02.920 --> 00:32:04.580
catalog that we could roll out.

00:32:04.580 --> 00:32:07.360
- I think this question
touches upon this as well,

00:32:07.360 --> 00:32:11.530
but George asked what systems are
you using to share data with people?

00:32:11.530 --> 00:32:14.160
Is that a different use
than what we're talking about now?

00:32:14.160 --> 00:32:16.990
- So a lot of it is
point to point transfer.

00:32:16.990 --> 00:32:19.870
We do just extract,
transfer, load. And

00:32:20.710 --> 00:32:23.320
we've got a number of our
systems talking to each other

00:32:23.320 --> 00:32:25.840
through APIs. But otherwise,

00:32:25.840 --> 00:32:28.630
we just have to figure out
all kinds of different ways to get

00:32:29.590 --> 00:32:32.155
the data sent to the
Data Science Environment that we're using.

00:32:33.790 --> 00:32:34.880
It's not always easy. I mean,

00:32:34.880 --> 00:32:36.190
we were at the point
where they were emailing--

00:32:36.190 --> 00:32:37.930
or they're not emailing
but where they were physically

00:32:37.930 --> 00:32:38.725
mailing hard drives

00:32:39.550 --> 00:32:42.430
and we're trying to get
away from that obviously. [CHUCKLES]

00:32:44.050 --> 00:32:47.920
- An anonymous question,
interesting question on Slido is,

00:32:47.920 --> 00:32:51.850
how does your team build
their own professional portfolios for

00:32:51.850 --> 00:32:54.040
when they may move
on to a new job?

00:32:54.040 --> 00:32:57.310
Is how can they show what
they've done without sharing protected info?

00:32:58.360 --> 00:33:00.900
- Yeah, we've talked
about that a lot. And

00:33:01.960 --> 00:33:05.620
the way I've kind of treated
the work my team does and

00:33:05.620 --> 00:33:07.630
because I've been in
the position I've been in--

00:33:07.630 --> 00:33:09.530
even though this is a new team,

00:33:09.530 --> 00:33:11.710
a lot of the team I'm working
with was one I did worked with

00:33:11.710 --> 00:33:14.140
for 3 and 1/2 years in Army
Talent Management and with army

00:33:14.140 --> 00:33:15.190
people analytics.

00:33:15.190 --> 00:33:17.320
And then a couple of
years prior to that in G1.

00:33:17.320 --> 00:33:19.120
I treat all my new hires

00:33:22.550 --> 00:33:24.020
like they're grad
students coming in.

00:33:24.020 --> 00:33:25.790
And they're going to come.
They're going to explore the

00:33:25.790 --> 00:33:27.830
data. They're going to get
familiar with the data sets we

00:33:27.830 --> 00:33:30.600
use, get familiar with
the business processes.

00:33:30.600 --> 00:33:34.505
And then they will find something
that works as a thesis project.

00:33:35.400 --> 00:33:35.900
So

00:33:36.510 --> 00:33:37.970
what is the thing
that grabs them.

00:33:37.970 --> 00:33:39.440
What is the thing that's
the most interesting.

00:33:39.440 --> 00:33:41.890
What's most suited to
their capabilities. And

00:33:42.590 --> 00:33:45.350
we've really built
things around that.

00:33:46.520 --> 00:33:51.035
We've been lucky in that there's been
so much to do that we could

00:33:52.730 --> 00:33:55.550
align people to the projects
that they are most interested in.

00:33:55.550 --> 00:33:58.460
The thing that we're trying to
work out is what happens if we

00:33:58.460 --> 00:34:01.880
have projects that are prioritized
that don't have the same grab.

00:34:03.170 --> 00:34:04.700
But that's kind
of how I have it.

00:34:04.700 --> 00:34:07.250
I have this whole laundry list of
things that I want to get after.

00:34:07.250 --> 00:34:07.750
And

00:34:08.239 --> 00:34:11.210
like your grad advisor did
with their research projects like,

00:34:11.210 --> 00:34:12.920
hey, let me put you
in this direction.

00:34:12.920 --> 00:34:14.179
Go research this a little bit.

00:34:14.179 --> 00:34:17.120
Develop this program or this
problem a little bit more and

00:34:17.120 --> 00:34:18.290
let's see if that grabs you.

00:34:18.290 --> 00:34:22.092
- Yeah, that's an interesting
idea, like the thesis project for

00:34:22.920 --> 00:34:23.760
new people coming in.

00:34:23.760 --> 00:34:26.429
And I see there's a
conversation happening in the

00:34:26.429 --> 00:34:27.645
chat as well about--

00:34:29.869 --> 00:34:30.369
well,

00:34:31.260 --> 00:34:33.750
adding on to that idea of
quick wins. And then, Alan,

00:34:33.750 --> 00:34:36.600
I saw you mentioned something
about how you do onboarding

00:34:36.600 --> 00:34:38.489
where people can
contribute right away.

00:34:38.489 --> 00:34:40.845
Would you want to jump
in here and share that?

00:34:42.590 --> 00:34:43.090
- Sure.

00:34:43.639 --> 00:34:45.449
I don't have a ton to add.

00:34:45.449 --> 00:34:48.830
We just really try
conscientiously to think about

00:34:48.830 --> 00:34:52.010
as someone's coming onto the
team or someone's joining the organization,

00:34:52.010 --> 00:34:55.070
how do we set them up
so that they have something meaningful

00:34:55.070 --> 00:34:57.950
to contribute as soon as
possible so they don't feel

00:34:57.950 --> 00:35:01.940
like they're waiting to become
credible for a year before

00:35:01.940 --> 00:35:03.390
people feel like
they can contribute.

00:35:03.390 --> 00:35:06.500
And so it's just really
important to give people ways

00:35:06.500 --> 00:35:08.495
to feel successful soon.

00:35:09.230 --> 00:35:10.130
And Kris,

00:35:10.130 --> 00:35:13.970
also reacting to that incoming
grad student kind of approach.

00:35:13.970 --> 00:35:16.400
I think that's such an
interesting model to set that

00:35:16.400 --> 00:35:19.250
up to say, explore this.
Find something interesting,

00:35:19.250 --> 00:35:22.145
dig into it. We'll see if we
can make a project out of it.

00:35:22.145 --> 00:35:25.800
It is really cool. And also
appreciate the dilemma of like,

00:35:25.800 --> 00:35:29.585
OK, we have to prioritize
that against the portfolio as well.

00:35:30.990 --> 00:35:31.262
- No,

00:35:31.262 --> 00:35:32.990
I like the idea of
incorporating the quick wins

00:35:32.990 --> 00:35:34.790
too because that's
basically the kind of stuff

00:35:35.450 --> 00:35:36.890
that new people
could get after.

00:35:37.880 --> 00:35:39.970
This one is--

00:35:39.970 --> 00:35:42.240
I hate using the term
low-hanging fruit. But this

00:35:42.240 --> 00:35:45.960
one's primed, ready to go.
Jump on this, chew on it,

00:35:45.960 --> 00:35:48.040
use it to get familiar
with the data,

00:35:48.040 --> 00:35:50.520
and then we go and
develop from there.

00:35:50.520 --> 00:35:53.475
- OK, I might need help
with this acronym maybe.

00:35:54.030 --> 00:35:57.540
A Slido question is, how
have you navigated OPM

00:35:57.540 --> 00:36:00.660
requirements as you seek
to modernize recruitment and

00:36:01.350 --> 00:36:03.150
retainment strategies?

00:36:03.150 --> 00:36:04.890
- Yeah, Office of
Personnel Management.

00:36:04.890 --> 00:36:07.920
So we really just
had to get into,

00:36:07.920 --> 00:36:09.510
the same way we did
in the military side,

00:36:09.510 --> 00:36:11.220
the letter of the
law. It's like,

00:36:11.220 --> 00:36:15.270
what do these things actually say
that we must do and what is

00:36:15.270 --> 00:36:17.200
written in DOD, or army policy,

00:36:17.940 --> 00:36:20.280
or something that's just
more of like a legacy.

00:36:20.280 --> 00:36:22.980
We really try to go back
to some of these things and talk

00:36:22.980 --> 00:36:24.810
about what the ground truth is.

00:36:26.190 --> 00:36:28.960
We haven't really
had much of an issue

00:36:29.760 --> 00:36:32.730
modernizing at least on the
data side because of all the

00:36:32.730 --> 00:36:34.755
direct hire authorities
that we have.

00:36:35.550 --> 00:36:38.535
We've really tried
to push also towards

00:36:39.870 --> 00:36:43.020
doing more with term hires,
which scares people in government.

00:36:43.020 --> 00:36:43.540
They're like, oh,

00:36:43.540 --> 00:36:45.300
you mean they're not going to
make this a career. It's like,

00:36:45.300 --> 00:36:48.030
people working in this space
don't want to spend their

00:36:48.030 --> 00:36:49.305
lifetime in the
same job anyway.

00:36:49.875 --> 00:36:51.330
They're going to come in.
They're going to do a thing.

00:36:51.330 --> 00:36:52.650
They're going to
pick a next thing.

00:36:52.650 --> 00:36:55.673
And you can see
if that piece is--

00:36:57.720 --> 00:37:00.180
that particular position is
validated at the same level.

00:37:00.180 --> 00:37:01.950
Because the projects mature.

00:37:01.950 --> 00:37:06.300
You might need some entry level
folks or you might need some

00:37:06.300 --> 00:37:08.940
folks to do data development
and data cleaning.

00:37:08.940 --> 00:37:09.960
And then after that,

00:37:09.960 --> 00:37:11.640
you'll realize you've got a
different use for that data,

00:37:11.640 --> 00:37:12.390
different project,

00:37:12.390 --> 00:37:14.500
he composition of your project
team is going to change.

00:37:14.500 --> 00:37:18.975
So we've really tried to use
the most flexible processes we have

00:37:20.130 --> 00:37:22.770
within the existing policies.

00:37:22.770 --> 00:37:25.440
But we're not afraid to go
up to OSD and say, hey, guys,

00:37:25.440 --> 00:37:27.120
we might want to rewrite
this thing a little bit.

00:37:27.120 --> 00:37:29.140
Look at what we could
do if you rewrite it.

00:37:29.140 --> 00:37:30.810
Here's our proposed
language. Just sign.

00:37:30.810 --> 00:37:31.700
- [CHUCKLES]

00:37:33.480 --> 00:37:34.690
I noticed in your

00:37:35.370 --> 00:37:36.900
bio you talk about

00:37:38.100 --> 00:37:41.130
your team conducting research
and doing experiments to

00:37:41.130 --> 00:37:45.570
prototype as well. And I
was wondering how do you create

00:37:45.570 --> 00:37:49.976
that culture where it's OK
to do these experiments?

00:37:51.730 --> 00:37:53.970
Just love to hear a
little bit about that.

00:37:53.970 --> 00:37:55.170
- Getting leader buy-out.

00:37:55.770 --> 00:37:57.870
Let's not throw a bunch of
money at it. Let's try it first.

00:37:57.870 --> 00:38:00.990
I can try it for you for
free and you can figure out how much

00:38:00.990 --> 00:38:03.180
money you want to spend
on the result. I mean,

00:38:03.180 --> 00:38:05.700
some of them we're looking
at different data integration into

00:38:05.700 --> 00:38:06.420
selection boards.

00:38:07.770 --> 00:38:09.270
As we are doing that,

00:38:09.270 --> 00:38:12.510
we just have the board members
stay on a couple of extra days,

00:38:12.510 --> 00:38:13.890
do a quick rundown with them,

00:38:13.890 --> 00:38:16.230
and pilot some of the new
look and feel of the board,

00:38:16.230 --> 00:38:18.090
and see how much
it changes their

00:38:18.930 --> 00:38:20.640
voting from the
previous version.

00:38:20.640 --> 00:38:22.020
And we are looking
at things like

00:38:23.060 --> 00:38:24.780
voter interreliability.

00:38:24.780 --> 00:38:28.320
We're able to explore things that
we haven't been able to explore before.

00:38:28.320 --> 00:38:31.470
And then we bring the
findings back. I mean, again,

00:38:31.470 --> 00:38:32.370
looking at the quick wins,

00:38:32.370 --> 00:38:34.350
we find the things that
are legit findings,

00:38:34.350 --> 00:38:36.120
bring those back to senior
leaders and go, hey, guys,

00:38:36.120 --> 00:38:38.820
we tested this but look at
all this other stuff we found.

00:38:38.820 --> 00:38:40.770
There's more experiments.
Let's go after this.

00:38:41.330 --> 00:38:43.680
And we get them excited
about it. We hype it up.

00:38:44.600 --> 00:38:45.890
And some of it's like,

00:38:45.890 --> 00:38:51.020
it's not necessarily world changing right
from the get go. But again,

00:38:51.020 --> 00:38:53.060
the teaching and communicating
piece we were talking about

00:38:53.060 --> 00:38:56.210
before. We got to sell
them on like, hey,

00:38:56.210 --> 00:38:58.760
this is just step one.
This is just version one.

00:38:58.760 --> 00:39:00.725
If we keep developing this,
think about what we could do.

00:39:01.460 --> 00:39:03.380
- I love that, that messaging.

00:39:04.670 --> 00:39:05.170
That's great.

00:39:05.930 --> 00:39:09.720
Caitlin, I see you had
a question here in the chat.

00:39:09.720 --> 00:39:11.930
I was thinking it might
be helpful for you to introduce

00:39:11.930 --> 00:39:12.815
yourself as well.

00:39:12.815 --> 00:39:13.920
[CHUCKLES]

