feel free to raise your hand
here on Zoom or maybe even just

00:47:28.255 --> 00:47:32.470
copy and paste those questions over just
so we see them again there too.

00:47:33.550 --> 00:47:34.960
KRIS SALING: More if the
chat's rocking and rolling.

00:47:34.960 --> 00:47:38.125
- Yeah, we always have a
big party there in the chat.

00:47:40.270 --> 00:47:43.630
Caitlin, I think you had
another question a little bit earlier.

00:47:43.630 --> 00:47:45.790
- Yeah. I just wanted
to ask, are there any

00:47:46.530 --> 00:47:49.330
impacts that the army has
changed due to the work with

00:47:49.330 --> 00:47:52.090
this new team that you
have or maybe people analytics that

00:47:52.090 --> 00:47:55.660
team that we see in the
army today that are directly from

00:47:55.660 --> 00:47:56.590
the work that you were doing?

00:47:58.440 --> 00:47:58.450
-

00:47:58.450 --> 00:48:01.640
I think the biggest one is
just getting rid of the additional

00:48:01.640 --> 00:48:03.120
selection boards. I mean,

00:48:03.120 --> 00:48:06.500
we've taken those and that's about
up to eight weeks of time

00:48:06.500 --> 00:48:08.210
where we have a bunch of
GOs sitting in a room. It's just

00:48:08.210 --> 00:48:12.290
like, OK, they're going into
an assessment based on this

00:48:12.290 --> 00:48:15.170
invitation that's going to
get the in-depth detail.

00:48:15.170 --> 00:48:16.640
And they're going to
go into interviews.

00:48:16.640 --> 00:48:18.200
They're going to go
into all these things.

00:48:18.200 --> 00:48:20.760
The file scrub, the automatic

00:48:21.590 --> 00:48:23.980
scoring of these things that

00:48:25.310 --> 00:48:27.270
they spent all this time on,
they don't need to do that.

00:48:27.270 --> 00:48:29.450
We can automate that.
And we automate it.

00:48:29.450 --> 00:48:32.090
We validate it with the
branches to make sure it makes

00:48:32.090 --> 00:48:33.740
sense. We get that
human back in the loop.

00:48:33.740 --> 00:48:36.470
But that's going to free up a
ton of time but it has also

00:48:36.470 --> 00:48:39.860
uncovered a lot of things that
we need to do with performance management.

00:48:40.760 --> 00:48:45.140
Otherwise, I mean, just
the introduction of the two-way

00:48:45.140 --> 00:48:48.890
market that HRC is using
to preference assignments.

00:48:49.750 --> 00:48:50.960
We're about to

00:48:51.590 --> 00:48:54.800
pilot a randomized controlled
trial for retention incentives

00:48:54.800 --> 00:48:59.160
using the retention prediction model
that we have as kind of

00:48:59.160 --> 00:49:01.290
an a priori statistical
indicator whether or not

00:49:01.290 --> 00:49:02.490
someone is likely to attrit.

00:49:02.490 --> 00:49:04.710
And then if they take one
of these incentives and sign an

00:49:04.710 --> 00:49:07.860
additional duty service
obligation, we know that's a

00:49:07.860 --> 00:49:12.240
win. We can get a statistical
lift prediction from these

00:49:12.240 --> 00:49:16.180
pilots on how likely these
different incentives are to keep people.

00:49:16.180 --> 00:49:18.270
So I think once we
get that rolled out,

00:49:18.270 --> 00:49:19.900
it's going to have
a lot of impact.

00:49:19.900 --> 00:49:22.470
But we're at the point
where we've got the model built.

00:49:22.470 --> 00:49:24.690
We've got the retention
incentives picked.

00:49:26.250 --> 00:49:28.140
Now it's time to
do the experiment.

00:49:28.140 --> 00:49:29.740
- Great, that's awesome.

00:49:29.740 --> 00:49:32.040
I'm really looking forward to
seeing all of these changes

00:49:32.040 --> 00:49:34.320
happen for the army.
Also, actually,

00:49:34.320 --> 00:49:36.180
for those on the call today,

00:49:36.180 --> 00:49:38.610
I work part time for the
Texas Army National Guard.

00:49:38.610 --> 00:49:42.390
So I definitely have a personal
side interest in all the work

00:49:42.390 --> 00:49:45.060
that Kris is doing.
So thank you.

00:49:46.040 --> 00:49:47.230
KRIS SALING: No, thank you.

00:49:47.230 --> 00:49:47.450
- Kris,

00:49:47.450 --> 00:49:50.600
I thought it might be
interesting to learn a little

00:49:50.600 --> 00:49:53.255
bit more about your
own personal journey

00:49:54.095 --> 00:49:55.520
in getting into this role

00:49:56.270 --> 00:49:57.330
of leading the HRC

00:49:58.070 --> 00:50:01.170
2030 vision. And I
was wondering like was

00:50:02.030 --> 00:50:06.440
there a specific moment or
something that led you to

00:50:06.440 --> 00:50:09.350
wanting to lead this initiative?

00:50:10.820 --> 00:50:13.070
- It was an
interesting ramp up.

00:50:13.070 --> 00:50:15.510
Because my undergrad was
in operations research.

00:50:16.190 --> 00:50:18.170
I commissioned as an engineer
so I really didn't touch

00:50:18.170 --> 00:50:19.865
anything until I went
back to grad school.

00:50:20.780 --> 00:50:23.310
I went back to grad
school and studied complexity science.

00:50:23.310 --> 00:50:27.020
So I was doing
rare event simulation, probabilistic design,

00:50:27.020 --> 00:50:29.480
stuff that doesn't really
come into the people enterprise.

00:50:29.480 --> 00:50:31.340
Went and taught that at
West Point for a while.

00:50:31.340 --> 00:50:34.340
And then my first job as an
operations research and systems

00:50:34.340 --> 00:50:37.430
analyst was working theater
security cooperation and

00:50:37.430 --> 00:50:38.780
building partner capacity,

00:50:38.780 --> 00:50:41.290
counterterrorism
stuff for INDOPACOM.

00:50:42.050 --> 00:50:44.970
Then my branch pulled a
fast one on me and said,

00:50:44.970 --> 00:50:47.130
we're going to send you to
the Army G1. I was like, what

00:50:47.130 --> 00:50:48.300
are you sending me
to G1 for?

00:50:48.300 --> 00:50:50.790
I don't know anything
about HR. I don't know.

00:50:50.790 --> 00:50:52.740
I'm not a personnel
analyst. OK, well,

00:50:52.740 --> 00:50:53.880
I guess I better
figure this out.

00:50:53.880 --> 00:50:55.200
And that

00:50:56.070 --> 00:50:58.260
was the best thing that
could have happened to me,

00:50:58.260 --> 00:51:00.870
because I was the Napoleon's
corporal sitting there in the

00:51:00.870 --> 00:51:02.490
corner going, why do
you do this? What is it?

00:51:02.490 --> 00:51:04.830
This doesn't make sense.
Why is this thing here?

00:51:04.830 --> 00:51:06.780
Why are we using Excel
spreadsheets where we've got an

00:51:06.780 --> 00:51:08.250
environment where you
can house all this--

00:51:08.250 --> 00:51:09.820
oh, because it doesn't
have the data in it.

00:51:09.820 --> 00:51:11.370
How do I get the
data put in it? And

00:51:12.525 --> 00:51:16.260
this is kind of the
culmination of eight years of curiosity,

00:51:16.260 --> 00:51:19.080
and building things,
and asking questions,

00:51:19.080 --> 00:51:20.820
and trying to get
things simulated,

00:51:20.820 --> 00:51:24.960
and big problems in a space
that wasn't really heavy in the

00:51:24.960 --> 00:51:27.720
data sphere being
answered by data.

00:51:27.720 --> 00:51:31.080
And now we've got Secretary's
focus on it. We have

00:51:31.770 --> 00:51:34.920
a lot of different focuses
coming in that are leading us

00:51:34.920 --> 00:51:35.955
to be more data driven.

00:51:36.600 --> 00:51:40.110
And when the opportunity came up
to not just keep working on it--

00:51:40.110 --> 00:51:42.940
Because I started people analytics. Then
it's like, all right, we got--

00:51:42.940 --> 00:51:44.280
I didn't start it
completely from scratch.

00:51:44.280 --> 00:51:47.010
We took the human capital big
data program and rolled it up

00:51:47.010 --> 00:51:48.840
under an existing office
that was actually going to

00:51:50.460 --> 00:51:54.390
make use and figure out how
to build some serious models out

00:51:54.390 --> 00:51:55.575
of some of this
data we were using.

00:51:56.160 --> 00:51:58.380
I did the same thing when
I was at talent management.

00:51:58.380 --> 00:52:01.320
I built a little startup within
there that was churning out all

00:52:01.320 --> 00:52:03.360
these data products. And to me,

00:52:03.360 --> 00:52:05.840
this was the opportunity to
do the same thing within HRC,

00:52:05.840 --> 00:52:09.060
is to sell my other startup
to corporate and I'm going to go

00:52:09.060 --> 00:52:11.100
over and do this
other little startup.

00:52:11.100 --> 00:52:14.520
- I love just thinking of you
asking all those questions

00:52:14.520 --> 00:52:16.470
there, like why are we
doing it this way. And

00:52:17.190 --> 00:52:20.220
it's making me think of a
question I love to ask people.

00:52:20.220 --> 00:52:25.260
Is there a piece of career
advice that you've either given

00:52:25.260 --> 00:52:28.860
or received that you'd
like to share with us?

00:52:28.860 --> 00:52:29.055
-

00:52:29.055 --> 00:52:31.980
It's a tricky one to give
especially in the army where

00:52:31.980 --> 00:52:36.150
your first five to eight
years, it's all about conformity and

00:52:36.150 --> 00:52:38.490
compliance. And after that,

00:52:38.490 --> 00:52:41.010
all of a sudden they expect you
to flip a switch and go, OK,

00:52:41.010 --> 00:52:42.930
now you're in charge
of things, be creative.

00:52:42.930 --> 00:52:45.090
And there are a lot of people
who just get gummed up in the

00:52:45.090 --> 00:52:46.440
works. They don't
know how to do it.

00:52:47.930 --> 00:52:49.070
I tell people to find--

00:52:49.070 --> 00:52:51.140
we were talking about the
hobbies and the other things

00:52:51.140 --> 00:52:55.620
that we will do to
exercise our creative energy, those opposite

00:52:56.600 --> 00:52:57.800
mental muscles.

00:52:57.800 --> 00:53:01.260
And I just tell people,
it's going to sound silly,

00:53:01.260 --> 00:53:03.650
but exercise your
creative pursuits.

00:53:03.650 --> 00:53:09.770
Find a way to exercise that
creative energy so that you can keep

00:53:10.460 --> 00:53:11.555
the brain flexible.

00:53:13.020 --> 00:53:15.830
Especially if you want to
go into spaces like this where

00:53:15.830 --> 00:53:18.140
it's not charted,
it's very ambiguous.

00:53:18.140 --> 00:53:19.520
There's not a whole lot of--

00:53:19.520 --> 00:53:22.320
I mean, the last three
jobs I had didn't exist.

00:53:22.320 --> 00:53:24.860
They were created and I got
stuck in them. And there's no

00:53:24.860 --> 00:53:26.630
book. There's no notes.

00:53:26.630 --> 00:53:30.380
There's no anything about how
to do this particular job.

00:53:30.380 --> 00:53:31.760
I mean, so I had to go out--

00:53:31.760 --> 00:53:33.600
Just like we were
talking about before,

00:53:33.600 --> 00:53:34.880
if you're not there
to answer a question,

00:53:34.880 --> 00:53:36.560
the person's going to
go find the answers.

00:53:36.560 --> 00:53:38.870
So I'm going to go out
and talk to other HR professionals and

00:53:38.870 --> 00:53:39.590
see how they're doing this.

00:53:39.590 --> 00:53:41.630
I'm going to go talk
to other data science professionals and

00:53:41.630 --> 00:53:43.400
see how they're doing
this and put together

00:53:44.450 --> 00:53:48.480
a hodgepodge answer to
try out and experiment.

00:53:48.480 --> 00:53:51.790
And I mean this is just
a great place for it.

00:53:51.790 --> 00:53:53.550
And I saw it pop
up in the comments,

00:53:53.550 --> 00:53:55.560
data science is a great
place to be curious.

00:53:56.520 --> 00:53:57.690
If I were in my home office,

00:53:57.690 --> 00:54:00.780
I have a poster of Sherlock
Holmes from the movie up behind

00:54:00.780 --> 00:54:03.120
because he's always going,
data, data, data. I need data.

00:54:03.120 --> 00:54:06.870
I need to solve problems. I
want to go and dig into this.

00:54:06.870 --> 00:54:07.800
I want to solve a case.

00:54:08.470 --> 00:54:10.575
I'm going to be a
data detective. So

00:54:11.940 --> 00:54:13.080
it just gets you excited.

00:54:13.080 --> 00:54:15.195
I mean, find a way to exercise
those creative pursuits

00:54:15.840 --> 00:54:16.740
because they're fun. And

00:54:18.060 --> 00:54:21.120
I think you'll use those to
find that kind of creative fun

00:54:21.120 --> 00:54:22.320
in your work also.

00:54:22.320 --> 00:54:24.315
- I love that. Thank
you, Kris. And I can

00:54:25.050 --> 00:54:30.250
hear the excitement from you as
you're explaining that too. I love it.

00:54:30.250 --> 00:54:31.440
KRIS SALING: I love
my job. I won't lie.

00:54:31.440 --> 00:54:32.280
[CHUCKLING]

00:54:33.900 --> 00:54:33.945
-

00:54:33.945 --> 00:54:36.090
I know we have a few
minutes here but one other question

00:54:36.090 --> 00:54:37.560
that was asked anonymously was,

00:54:37.560 --> 00:54:40.830
how do you stay updated
with the latest and greatest tech

00:54:40.830 --> 00:54:43.230
that you can use and
enable your team with?

00:54:44.670 --> 00:54:47.120
- Conversations like
this. Getting out to--

00:54:47.900 --> 00:54:49.730
I've made the argument
that the army--

00:54:49.730 --> 00:54:51.830
because we pulled back
on conferences for a while.

00:54:51.830 --> 00:54:53.510
We need to go to
professional conferences.

00:54:53.510 --> 00:54:55.400
We need to go to
industry conferences.

00:54:55.400 --> 00:54:57.930
We need to go talk to people
who are doing this work.

00:54:57.930 --> 00:54:59.150
We need to go back to school.

00:54:59.150 --> 00:55:01.280
We need to go do
training with industry.

00:55:02.810 --> 00:55:06.530
We need to be able to go
and find the people who are leading

00:55:06.530 --> 00:55:09.650
the edge and create those
networks so when we do have a

00:55:09.650 --> 00:55:12.860
problem and we're like OK, what
is the industry doing, we go,

00:55:12.860 --> 00:55:14.030
who's leading in this edge.

00:55:14.030 --> 00:55:16.640
What kind of technology can
we take or what do we need to

00:55:16.640 --> 00:55:19.350
develop and how do we do it,
that you've got to phone,

00:55:19.350 --> 00:55:20.930
a friend available. And

00:55:20.930 --> 00:55:22.700
I won't say I'm always the
most up to date.

00:55:22.700 --> 00:55:25.550
And I have a little bit of
a pass from the army just because

00:55:25.550 --> 00:55:28.280
a lot of our stuff is
legacy and it doesn't necessarily take

00:55:28.280 --> 00:55:30.780
the most cutting edge thing
to solve those problems.

00:55:30.780 --> 00:55:33.620
But I do like knowing what's going
on and where the field is going.

00:55:33.620 --> 00:55:35.990
- Absolutely. We keep a

00:55:36.830 --> 00:55:39.710
running list of books
and recommendations from

00:55:39.710 --> 00:55:40.370
Data Science

00:55:40.370 --> 00:55:41.990
Hangout. Leaders
and everybody here--

00:55:41.990 --> 00:55:45.140
well, when I say we, Libby
is actually the one doing it.

00:55:45.140 --> 00:55:48.090
So do you have any
recommendations for books or

00:55:48.090 --> 00:55:50.490
things you're reading
now we should check out?

00:55:50.490 --> 00:55:51.660
- I've got a whole
laundry list.

00:55:51.660 --> 00:55:52.624
[CHUCKLES]

00:55:54.070 --> 00:55:55.570
It's probably been
brought up a few times,

00:55:55.570 --> 00:55:57.390
but if you're doing any
kind of transformation and

00:55:57.390 --> 00:55:58.620
modernization,
The Unicorn

00:55:58.620 --> 00:55:59.880
Project is a must read.

00:56:00.840 --> 00:56:01.515
I'm reading

00:56:02.700 --> 00:56:03.990
Further, Faster,
and Far

00:56:03.990 --> 00:56:06.210
Less Drama
by Janice Fraser right now.

00:56:06.210 --> 00:56:08.940
It's about change
management and transforming organizations.

00:56:08.940 --> 00:56:10.230
That's another good one. And

00:56:10.230 --> 00:56:12.535
I got recommended Jeff
Baer's--

00:56:13.250 --> 00:56:15.390
I think that's who it
was by. It's called Hug Your

00:56:15.390 --> 00:56:18.210
Haters. And it's
about customer experience and

00:56:18.210 --> 00:56:21.390
customer service. But the reason I
love it is it teaches you to

00:56:22.170 --> 00:56:25.680
actively seek out criticism and
critics and find the people who

00:56:25.680 --> 00:56:26.610
are just making noise,

00:56:26.610 --> 00:56:29.670
but also find the ones who
have a legitimate complaint and

00:56:29.670 --> 00:56:31.900
figure out how you can solve
their problems for them.

00:56:31.900 --> 00:56:33.570
So I love that.

00:56:34.760 --> 00:56:36.220
- Thank you for reminding
me of that book.

00:56:36.220 --> 00:56:39.820
I heard of it a few months
ago and completely forgot.

00:56:39.820 --> 00:56:42.040
I need to go add
that to the list too.

00:56:42.040 --> 00:56:42.250
-

00:56:42.250 --> 00:56:44.950
I see lots of good detective
books popping up in the chat too.

00:56:44.950 --> 00:56:48.550
- Yeah, it seems like there's a
lot of us here who wanted to be

00:56:48.550 --> 00:56:50.620
detectives or Sherlock Holmes,

00:56:50.620 --> 00:56:52.570
Nancy Drew at some
point in our life.

00:56:52.570 --> 00:56:53.223
[CHUCKLES]

00:56:53.930 --> 00:56:55.180
So it probably has
some relation-- KRIS

00:56:55.180 --> 00:56:55.780
SALING: This is kind of what
we do. We're finding clues. RACHAEL

00:56:55.780 --> 00:56:57.305
DEMPSEY: --to
data. Yeah.

00:56:57.305 --> 00:56:57.805
- Yeah.

00:56:58.480 --> 00:56:59.170
- Absolutely.

00:56:59.170 --> 00:57:01.940
Well, thank you so much,
Kris, for joining us today.

00:57:01.940 --> 00:57:05.530
It's been a pleasure hearing
from you and learning from your

00:57:05.530 --> 00:57:08.050
experience. Thank you
for all that you do.

00:57:08.920 --> 00:57:10.210
KRIS SALING: Thank you so
much for letting me join.

00:57:10.210 --> 00:57:11.470
This is awesome.

00:57:11.470 --> 00:57:12.980
- Have a great rest
of the day everybody.

00:57:12.980 --> 00:57:14.170
Thanks so much for joining us.

00:57:14.810 --> 00:57:15.910
We'll see you all next week.

